WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Matt: Who is it?

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:02.120
W…Who’s there?

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:03.120
Monster: It’s me, Matthew.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.120
Matt: Who?

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.120
Monster: It’s you.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.120
Me.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.120
Matthew.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.120
Matt: Who are you?

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.120
Who are you!?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.990
Monster: It’s time for you to RECORD THE
EPISODE, Matthew.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:21.000
Matt: No!!

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:31.010
No, You stay away!

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:38.510
YOU STAY AWAY!

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.020
NO!!!

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:48.520
*MatPat Breathing heavily*

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.690
Hello Internet.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.510
Welcome to Film Theory, the show that definitely
isn't standing right behind you.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.140
If you've spent any time watching the Channel
recently, you know that we are in love with

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.820
the world of analog horror.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:05.220
There are countless incredible independent
filmmakers here on YouTube telling some of

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.910
the most compelling and scariest stories in
modern entertainment right now.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:13.750
I'm talking stuff like the Walton files, the
Backrooms, the Mandela catalog, stories that

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.830
all have an iconic look and feel because they're
largely set in the eighties, nineties and

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:18.880
early aughts.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.700
This was a time when video tech was first
becoming widely available and finding its

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:23.700
footing.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.470
Camcorders were giant boxes that you had to
cart around with you.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.520
and if you actually wanted to film something,
well, you also had to bring along a library's

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.740
worth of physical tapes to record on.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.740
No exaggeration.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:38.280
My family had a literal cooler sized bag just
to carry everything we needed to film, like

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.820
30 minutes of the family Christmas.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.299
And even then, the footage we got was ROUGH.

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:44.240
When something moved, the footage got blurry.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.229
When it was dark, it got grainy.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:49.729
Most of the time you just left what you shot
on the recording tapes because transferring

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:54.870
it to an actual VHS tape you could play required
a masters degree in arranging wires and cables

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.799
from the camcorder to the VCR.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:01:59.670
And then once you actually watched what you
made, your mileage varied.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:03.820
I’m guessing no one watching right now has
ever even heard of a tracking button on a

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.470
remote control before, rights?

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:06.720
That ringing bells for anyone?

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.950
Well, you know, those blurry lines that go
across the screen and pretty much all analog

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:10.950
horror uploads.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.670
Those were most often caused by a misalignment
of the tape inside the VHS and the playback

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.290
head inside the VCR.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:20.160
And tracking was the button that used to help
sync those two things up.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.030
This is the awkward puberty era of tech that
I'm talking about, and that's what I love

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.770
about these analog horror series.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.950
They're videos that take the early limitations
of tech from the era and use them to create

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.390
genuine, authentic, memorable scares.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.230
Now, at the top of the episode, you heard
that this video is being sponsored by the

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:36.230
Google Pixel 7 pro.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.209
In fact, that whole opening was filmed on
the phone's camera, a camera that just has

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.879
a ton of amazing features.

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:46.080
We had to work really hard to make the footage
from this thing look old and scary, like analog

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.080
horror.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:48.080
Just take a look at this.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.780
This is the final shot, but watch as we remove
one filter, two, get rid of the vignette and

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.879
a final effect.

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:53.879
Boom.

00:02:53.879 --> 00:02:57.090
This is the original footage that we were
working with and it looks fantastic.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.890
Which got me thinking what would modern analog
horror look like?

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:04.640
The esthetic of analog horror is built off
these technological limitations of eighties

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.120
and nineties home video: Motion blur, no ability
to see in the dark, things that are just barely

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.290
visible off in the distance.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.920
But those limitations don't exist today.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.959
Just look at the Google Pixel 7 pro, a phone
small enough to fit in your pocket, but equipped

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:20.680
with a camera smart enough to challenge tech
ten times its size.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.989
Motion blur isn't a problem because it has
image stabilization.

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:27.030
In fact, the blur is in your control with
features like Cinematic Blur, keeping the

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.010
subject in focus and blurring the background.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.069
Is it too dark?

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:31.069
Not a problem.

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.260
The Google Pixel 7 Pro has night sight, where
you can take a picture in almost absolute

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.180
darkness and still wind up with something
to show for it.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.549
Oh, is there something off in the distance
that's too far to truly make out?

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:43.090
Again, it's not a problem.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:48.500
This itty bitty piece of tech now has a five
time zoom telephoto lens and records in 4K,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.920
so that scary pile of pixels off in the woods
is just the non scary pile of rocks that it

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:53.920
always was.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.250
I gotta be honest with you, we have great
cameras here in the studio to film on, but

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.340
most of the shoots that we do at this point
are on pixel phones because the cameras are

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.049
high quality, they're easy to use and they're
smart.

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:08.020
The shots look better because the camera is
largely doing a lot of the work for us.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.879
So this then begs the question, is modern
technology too good to make effective analog

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:13.879
horror?

00:04:13.879 --> 00:04:17.630
Could an analog horror series take place in
the modern day when everyone in their grandmother

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.810
has access to a high quality camera sitting
in their pocket?

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.540
Today we're looking at the tropes of analog
horror to see what the equivalent would be

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.120
if it was themed around today.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.600
In 20 to 30 years.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.250
When people look back at this era of entertainment,
what will the retro tech horror stories look

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.250
and feel like?

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:37.180
Curl up and hide under the blankets loyal
theorists, we're about to find out.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.090
So before we can really jump into the idea
of modern tech being too good to make analog

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:44.120
horror, we should first take a step back to
fully understand what makes the eighties and

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.840
nineties tech so perfect for this type of
storytelling.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.020
Like I mentioned in the intro, series like
The Walton Files, Back Rooms, Mandela catalog,

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.050
all of them were made to look like they were
filmed on ancient camcorders.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:57.100
They have that iconic, jittery, blurry VHS
look.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.750
This sort of technology became cheap enough
to mass produced by the 1980s and were fairly

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.389
common by the 1990s.

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:06.030
That's why so many of these series take place
in this era of content.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.470
It's also where we get the “analog” part
of the label “analog horror”.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.880
These old camcorders used analog signals to
write information onto actual magnetic strips.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:17.870
An analog signal is basically some sort of
thing that constantly changes to represent

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:18.870
different data points.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.450
This could be the heights or frequencies of
wavelengths, like with radio signals.

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:23.450
or even stuff.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.910
That's simple, like a record where it reads
the heights of grooves on the record and then

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.250
translates that into an audio output.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:32.870
Because analog recordings are literal waves,
there's an infinite amount of detail that

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.630
you get in there, but it also makes it more
likely to have interference like you see with

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.630
television static.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:41.440
That's why old TVs had antennas that you could
use to fine tune the signal.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.960
By contrast, digital signals are discrete
packages that are stored as units of ones

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:46.960
and zeros.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.919
A digital recording takes snapshots of the
analog signal at a certain rate, the higher

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:54.600
rate, the closer to the original signal you're
going to get instead of encoding information

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.220
onto a physical tape like with analog, digital
signals store it onto memory cards.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.520
Anyway, at that time, analog tapes had the
potential to be decent quality.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.850
But it turns out that the tech wasn't exactly
the ideal solution for long term storage.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:11.240
See, magnetic tape begins to degrade after
10 to 20 years if it isn't stored properly,

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:12.780
meaning the footage gets blurry, it

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.310
gets glitchy, corrupted.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:18.060
Here's a friendly reminder to convert all
your old home videos into digital if you haven't

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.069
already here, folks.

00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:22.430
What's more, the footage from these old cameras
and camcorders was often interlaced.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:26.419
Have you ever pause an old video and noticed
that any motion blur seems to have this weird

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.569
series of horizontal lines running through
it?

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:30.480
That means that the footage was interlaced.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.720
Each single frame of the video only had about
half the information needed for the full image.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.810
The rest of the image used the previous frame,
meaning that any motion would be slightly

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.919
offset and thus the horizontal lines.

00:06:40.919 --> 00:06:45.190
This drastically cut the amount of data needed
for the full video, making it easier to both

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:46.220
store and broadcast.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.569
But it also resulted in huge losses in quality.

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:53.569
The opposite of this is known as Progressive
Scan, where an entire images worth of video

00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:55.289
is shown in each individual frame.

00:06:55.289 --> 00:06:58.220
You ever notice when you select the quality
of a YouTube video, it says something like

00:06:58.220 --> 00:06:59.220
1080P?

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.789
Well, the “P” stands for Progressive Scan,
and almost all video shot today uses that

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:04.870
as the standard.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:09.250
Regardless, the lower quality interlaced video
being played on a big old CRT screen helped

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.410
to create the iconic, flickering look that
we now

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:13.850
associate with old videos from this era.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:17.259
And you see, that's why it works so well for
horror made on a budget.

00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:21.720
Not only is this sort of technology really
cheap nowadays, but the look is easy to recreate

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.310
in post-production using modern editing software.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:28.090
Like here, our editors just threw a bunch
of effects on this to make it look like an

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.160
old VHS.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.639
And it's, what, like two layers in after effects?

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:35.890
This lets young filmmakers create something
that's immediately stylish, atmospheric, set

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.720
in a clear time and place in history.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.440
What's more important, though, is that they're
also able to use this esthetic to amplify

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.289
what makes the series scary.

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:45.540
This look creates an intentional lack of clarity.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:49.530
See, when you introduce a scary idea, but
you don't show that scary thing.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.159
What your audience imagines in their head
is far more horrifying than anything you could

00:07:53.159 --> 00:07:54.620
possibly come up with yourself.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.199
It doesn't matter what medium you're in.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:08:00.940
The idea is used to great effect in the Cthulhu
Mythos books, and a ton of the most iconic

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.690
horror movies of all time have embraced this
exact idea.

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:07.159
Is your mechanical shark animatronic not working?

00:08:07.159 --> 00:08:08.159
Don't worry.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:11.629
Just don't show the shark for the majority
of the movie and it's going to wind up being

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:14.639
way more effective and way scarier.

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:18.949
And it's this lack of clarity that really
intensifies the actual horror that analog

00:08:18.949 --> 00:08:21.810
horror is based on that what's familiar can
be deadly.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.960
Look at the three series that I keep using
as examples.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.620
The Mandela catalog is all about our friends
and neighbors being impostors.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:32.010
The Walton Files is about a family friendly
burger restaurant that turns deadly, and the

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.600
back rooms is about the horror of mundane
walls and fluorescent lights.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.169
These aren't supernatural creatures.

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:42.100
These are series that rely on the normal people
and places around us, but made unnatural and

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.100
scary.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:46.330
This idea that the familiar can hurt you is
probably best exemplified by another trend

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.230
that came out of this era.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.230
Stranger danger.

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:53.000
Though the term dates back all the way to
the 1960s, it didn't catch fire until 1979,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.200
when a child in Manhattan was tragically kidnaped
while walking to school.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:01.570
News media grabbed hold of the story, who
spread it nationwide and soon began widely

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.320
reporting similar cases of missing kids.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:09.130
This culminated in 1982, when it was widely
reported that nearly 50,000 American children

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.480
were being kidnaped by strangers every year.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.460
And just so we're crystal clear on this, that
number is wildly inflated, but it managed

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:20.450
to cement the idea that all strangers can
be potentially dangerous in the minds of the

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:21.450
nation.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:24.530
And there were PSAs and school assemblies
drilling that idea into people throughout

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.390
the eighties and nineties, even up through
the mid 2000s.

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:32.350
But these analog horror series also play off
another innate human fear, the uncanny valley.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:35.950
This is a phenomenon that many psychologists
have noticed where people have strong negative

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.380
reactions to things that look almost human
but

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.320
aren't quite all the way there.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.280
Insert any low budget, poorly executed CGI
here.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.910
But again, this is where analog horror shines.

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:50.790
Without the budget to pay for pristine, photorealistic
computer generated worlds, these stories lean

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.670
into the unsettling nature of things that
are slightly off from reality.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.730
The alternates from Mandela catalog are stretched,
deformed human monsters.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.940
They're almost human, but not quite.

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:06.110
The animatronics of Walton's restaurants exhibit
jerky movements and speak in dead, emotionless,

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.339
prerecorded phrases.

00:10:07.339 --> 00:10:11.330
The Backrooms takes the generic office layout,
but then twists it into something that's not

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.029
built by human hands.

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:15.019
Something that on the surface looks familiar.

00:10:15.019 --> 00:10:18.459
But where the nuances don't match up, the
layout doesn't quite work with what those

00:10:18.459 --> 00:10:22.420
spaces would actually look and feel like if
it was actually made by humans.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:25.680
Things are just slightly off, which keeps
us off balance and nervous.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:28.970
But having laid all of that out, you can start
to see the problem here, right?

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:33.620
Modern technology just isn't super compatible
with that flavor of classic analog horror.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.720
Our cameras are too good and there's just
a lot of them everywhere.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.900
And even if you are working with older, blurry
footage today, there are upscaling programs

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:45.350
that are able to use complex algorithms to
increase the quality of footage near flawlessly.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:49.080
So if we were to sit here and think about
what modern analog horror would be, what would

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:52.240
it look like if current analog horror thrives
on the limitations of

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:53.300
the technology from the past?

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.600
What will the creators of the future be?

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.990
Leading to scare us with stories about today.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.750
Since clarity of image isn't an issue anymore,
we need to find something about contemporary

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.440
technology that IS scary.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.920
Honestly, I had to really think about this
one.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:09.360
I mean, there are some obvious answers, like
social media and that everyone shares a ton

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.710
of information about themselves online and
not everyone is what they seem.

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:13.720
But that's not super unique.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.389
Those stories are already being told by Hollywood.

00:11:15.389 --> 00:11:17.350
We all understand the scare potential there.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.870
It's also not a true parallel to analog horror.

00:11:19.870 --> 00:11:24.339
Again, the genre is about the limitations
of filming equipment, equipment that today

00:11:24.339 --> 00:11:26.720
seems to have no limitation.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.350
And therein lies the answer, I think.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.490
That right there is the point.

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:34.040
Not that the video gets blurry in the dark
or that a shaky camera obscures the movement

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:35.529
of a monster as it comes towards you.

00:11:35.529 --> 00:11:39.639
In an ironic twist where analog horror, as
we know it's rooted in the limitations of

00:11:39.639 --> 00:11:41.070
technology from the eighties and nineties.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:45.720
I expect creators of analog horror in the
future to lean into the idea that technology

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:51.529
became too good in the 2020s, that what we
define as photo realism has actually evolved

00:11:51.529 --> 00:11:56.830
beyond reality now able to create realities
that never even existed in the first place.

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:58.990
What made me think of this was actually the
Pixel 7.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.240
There's this feature in the camera called
Magic Eraser, which lets you go into your

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.800
photos after you take them to remove small
objects in the background.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.620
It does this using AI, filling in the space
left by the object with information about

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:10.690
the space around it.

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:13.220
It is, without exaggeration, an incredible
tool.

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:16.880
It helps you to create the perfect moment,
even if you weren't able to capture it at

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:17.880
the time.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.602
I cannot tell you the amount of times that
Steph and I have woken up at 4 a.m. just to

00:12:20.602 --> 00:12:23.949
